   136	
   137	\begin{observation}\label{lem:variablegadget}
   138		Solely removing the green and orange tiles of a variable gadget results in a disconnected shape.
   139	\end{observation}
   140	
   141	As a consequence of \cref{lem:variablegadget}, the forced choice of removing either the green or the orange tiles, can be used to determine an assignment for the respective Boolean variable. It remains to show how a variable gadget can be deconstructed, if additional cuboids are attached at each side.
   142	
   143	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:variablegadgetdestruction}
   144		Let $P$ be a polycube that is put together by a variable gadget and one cuboid ($Q_3$ and $Q_4$) at each end, connected to the respective L-shaped parts. Then $P$ is only deconstructible if at least $Q_3$ or $Q_4$ is deconstructible.
   145	\end{lemma}
   146	
   147	\begin{proof}
   148		Without loss of generality, let $Q_3$ be deconstructible. Then the orange tiles can be removed in order to deconstruct $Q_4$ (by \cref{lem:decomposablewall}), and $Q_2$ afterwards. Due to the hole between $Q_1$ and $Q_3$ and the assumption that $Q_3$ is deconstructible, $Q_1$ can also be deconstructed. 
   149		
   150		On the other hand, if neither $Q_3$ nor $Q_4$ is deconstructible, only the green or the orange tiles can be removed (by \cref{lem:variablegadget}). But then, either $Q_3$ or $Q_4$ can be deconstructed, but not both, resulting in an indestructible shape.
   151	\end{proof}
   152	
   153	As the last ingredient for our NP-hardness reduction we need a gadget that realizes a conjunction.
   154	This gadget will be used to guarantee that a variable gadget can be completely deconstructed if and only if all clauses in which the respective variable participates are satisfied.
   155	
   156	\subparagraph*{Conjunction gadget.}  As illustrated in \cref{fig:conjunctiongadget}, the conjunction gadget is T-shaped. 
   157	The wall between the cuboids $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ contains teeth to both sides, whereas the wall at cuboid $Q_3$ has teeth except for the positions where the T-shape is connected. This connection will be the crucial part to deconstruct this gadget.
   158	At all three positions ($Q_1$, $Q_2$, and $Q_3$) we attach connector gadgets leading either to another conjunction, to a variable, or to a clause gadget. Note that these connector gadgets have the same size as the conjunction gadget, i.e., the solid layers of the connectors and the conjunction gadget match.
   159	
   160	\begin{figure}
   161			\centering
   162			\includegraphics[scale=0.032]{figures/AND_Gadget_1_gray_c.jpg}\hfill
   163			\includegraphics[scale=0.032]{figures/AND_Gadget_2_gray_c.jpg}\hfill
   164			\includegraphics[scale=0.032]{figures/AND_Gadget_3_gray_c.jpg}
   165		\caption{Different views on the conjunction gadget. At the indicated positions, three indestructible cuboids $Q_i$ are attached. The whole construction is deconstructible, if and only if both, $Q_1$ and $Q_2$, or $Q_3$ is deconstructible. This follows from the design of the respective teeth layers, as well as the dark green tiles.}
   166		\label{fig:conjunctiongadget}
   167	\end{figure}
   168	
   169	We can show that this gadget is deconstructible if and only if the cuboid at $Q_3$, or both cuboids at $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are deconstructible.
   170	
   171	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:conjunctiongadget}
   172		Let $P$ be a polycube that is put together by three cuboids $Q_1, Q_2,$ and $Q_3$ which are connected by a conjunction gadget. Then $P$ is deconstructible if and only if $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are both deconstructible, or $Q_3$ is deconstructible.
   173	\end{lemma}
   174	
   175	\begin{proof}
   176		For the proof, we distinguish two cases.
   177		
   178		First, let $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ be deconstructible. Thus, all teeth from the respective insides can be removed. After this, the wall that is shared by $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ becomes deconstructible. Afterwards, by removing the dark green tiles (see~\cref{fig:conjunctiongadget}), we create a hole to reach the inside of $Q_3$ that makes $Q_3$ deconstructible as well. Thus, $P$ is deconstructible.
   179		
   180		Now assume that either $Q_1$ or $Q_2$ is deconstructible, but not both. Without loss of generality consider $Q_1$ to be deconstructible. Then all teeth from its inside can be removed. By this, neither $Q_2$ nor $Q_3$ become deconstructible because the teeth on the respective sides do not permit the removal of enough tiles such that tiles from the respective insides can pass through. Thus, $P$ is not deconstructible.
   181		
   182		It remains to show that $P$ is deconstructible if $Q_3$ is deconstructible. Because $Q_3$ is deconstructible, we can remove the teeth at this side of the conjunction gadget. Because there are no teeth at the opposite sides, the whole wall can be deconstructed. This results in holes to the inside of $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ such that these cuboids become deconstructible as well.
   183	\end{proof}
   184	
   185	By putting all these together, we obtain the following.
   186	
   187	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:hardness}
   188		\textsc{3D-}\problemtitle is NP-complete.
   189	\end{theorem}
   190	
   191	
   192	\begin{proof}
   193		First we note that the problem of deciding whether or not a polycube is constructible is in NP. For this, we guess a permutation of the involved tiles and check if there is a feasible construction step for each tile. It is easy to see that a single construction step has length~$O(n)$. Because the target polycube has size $n$, a construction sequence has length $O(n^2)$.
   194		
   195		To show NP-hardness, consider a rectilinear planar embedding of the variable-clause incidence graph $G_{\varphi}$ of a given \textsc{Planar Monotone 3Sat} formula $\varphi$, where the variable vertices are placed horizontally in a row, and clauses containing unnegated and negated literals are placed above and below this row, respectively. 
   196		We place a variable gadget for every variable (white in \cref{fig:hardness-reduction}), and a cuboid for each clause (green in \cref{fig:hardness-reduction}). 
   197		These blocks are connected via connector cuboids (gray) and conjunction gadgets whenever the number of unnegated or negated occurrences of a variable is larger than one.
   198		On top of this construction, we use a frame to hold all clauses and L-shaped parts in the variable gadgets together. This will be necessary to deconstruct the shape completely whenever the underlying Boolean 3Sat formula is satisfiable.
   199		
   200		\begin{claim}
   201			If there is a deconstruction sequence $\widetilde\Sigma$ for $P_{\varphi}$, then there is a satisfying assignment for $\varphi$.
   202		\end{claim}
   203		
   204		In order to deconstruct a clause gadget, at least (parts of) one of its literal containing variable gadgets has to be already deconstructed. Thus, every deconstruction sequence has to begin with deconstruction steps that remove either the green or orange tiles from any variable gadget. As argued in \cref{lem:variablegadget}, the green and orange tiles cannot both be removed, i.e., setting a variable to true and false simultaneously is not possible. Because $\widetilde\Sigma$ is a deconstruction sequence, eventually each clause will be deconstructed.
   205		
   206		Therefore, there is a satisfying assignment for $\varphi$ given by the order in which each variable of $P_{\varphi}$ is deconstructed by $\widetilde\Sigma$. In particular, for each $x_i \in \varphi$, we set $x_i = 1$ if the respective green tiles are removed first, and $x_i = 0$ otherwise.
   207		
   208		\begin{claim}
   209			If the Boolean formula $\varphi$ is satisfiable, then $P_{\varphi}$ is deconstructible by some deconstruction sequence $\widetilde\Sigma$.
   210		\end{claim}
   211		
   212		Let $\alpha$ be a satisfying assignment of $\varphi$. Then the polycube $P_{\varphi}$ can be deconstructed as follows: According to whether a variable is set to true or false in $\alpha$, either the green or orange tiles are removed, respectively. These deconstruction steps produce holes, large enough for a deconstruction of the whole literal representing cube, as argued in \cref{lem:variablegadget}. Afterwards, because there is a hole from the clause cuboids to the literals, all clauses that contain these literals can be deconstructed by \cref{lem:decomposablewall}. Because $\alpha$ is a satisfying assignment for $\varphi$, all clause gadgets and the respective parts of the variable gadgets can be deconstructed. The respective parts of the variable gadgets that are not participating in the satisfying assignment are connected by the overlaying connectivity frame. Because the clauses are already deconstructed, there are holes through which the remaining parts of the variable gadgets can be deconstructed. Removing the connectivity frame results in deconstructing~$P_{\varphi}$.
   213		
   214		These two claims complete the proof.
   215	\end{proof}

===== 04-approx.tex =====
     1	\section{Optimization variant and approximation}
     2	
     3	For polyominoes and polycubes that cannot be constructed, it is natural to consider the problem of constructing a subshape of maximum size. 
     4	We show that for each shape $P$ of size~$n$ in dimension $d$, a portion of $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{(d-1)}{d}})$ can always be constructed, implying an $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{-1}{d}})$-approximation for \textsc{MaxSTAP}.
     5	
     6	\begin{figure}[h]
     7		\begin{subfigure}{0.33\textwidth}
     8			\includegraphics[page=1, scale=0.85]{figures/figs-final}
     9			\caption{}
    10			\label{fig:approx1}
    11		\end{subfigure}\hfill
    12		\begin{subfigure}{0.33\textwidth}
    13			\includegraphics[page=2, scale=0.85]{figures/figs-final}
    14			\caption{}
    15			\label{fig:approx2}
    16		\end{subfigure}\hfill
    17		\begin{subfigure}{0.33\textwidth}
    18			\includegraphics[page=3, scale=0.85]{figures/figs-final}
    19			\caption{}
    20			\label{fig:approx3}
    21		\end{subfigure}
    22		\caption{(a) Boundary tiles (dark cyan) and non-boundary tiles (gray). (b) Every step of the  algorithm adds a tile in two steps: (1)~Move a new tile $t$ to a boundary position $p$ of $P$ that is free. (2)~Move $t$ on $P$ from $p$ to a position that is adjacent to a tile of the current polyomino $P'$. (c) The curve $B$ (blue), and the set $T$ (orange).}
    23		\label{fig:approx}
    24	\end{figure}
    25	
    26	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:constructability}
    27		In dimension $d=2, 3$, the greedy algorithm is an $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{-1}{d}})$-approximation for \textsc{MaxSTAP}.
    28	\end{theorem}
    29	
    30	\begin{proof}
    31		We prove the theorem by showing that greedily filling up accessible free positions leads to a polyomino $P' \subseteq P$ with $\nicefrac{\vert P'\vert}{\vert P\vert} \in \Omega(n^{\nicefrac{-1}{d}})$. 
    32		A position $p$ is \emph{accessible} with respect to a polyomino $P$ if we can move a tile $t$ to this position such that $t$~is never adjacent to a tile of $P$ unless $t$ lies on $p$. A tile $p \in P$ is a \emph{boundary tile} of $P$ if $p$ is accessible with respect to $P \setminus p$, see \cref{fig:approx1}. 
    33		If there is a boundary tile $p$ of $P$ that is not part of $P'$, we add a new tile $t$ to $P'$ in two steps, see \cref{fig:approx2}: 
    34		(1)~We move $t$ to the position $p$. 
    35		(2)~We move $t$ on $P$ to a position adjacent to a tile of $P'$. This implies that the greedy algorithm ends up with a polyomino holding all boundary tiles of $P$.
    36		
    37		Next, we show a lower bound on the boundary tiles of $P$ of $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{(d-1)}{d}})$.
    38		We will only show this for dimension $d=2$, %a 
    39		similar %surface-volume-
    40		arguments hold for $d=3$. 
    41		Let $B$~be the union of all edges lying between an accessible and a non-accessible position with respect to $P$, see blue curve in \cref{fig:approx3}.
    42		$B$ is a non-self-intersecting curve by the definition of accessible positions. 
    43		Thus, $B$ partitions the plane into a bounded area $A$ containing $P$ and an unbounded area. 
    44		Let $T$ be the union of all positions from $A$ sharing at least a corner with $B$, see the orange positions in \cref{fig:approx3}. 
    45		Then $\vert T\vert \geq \sqrt{\vert A\vert}$. 
    46		Note that not each position of $T$ is occupied by~$P$, see the light gray positions in \cref{fig:approx3}. 
    47		Let $T'$ be the positions along $T$ that share an edge with $B$. It is easy to see that $2\vert T'\vert\geq \vert T\vert$.
    48		Each position $p$ from $T'$ that is not a boundary tile from $P$ is adjacent to a boundary tile $p'$ from $P$.
    49		We call $p'$ a \emph{blocking tile} of $p$. 
    50		Each boundary tile is a blocking tile for a constant number of positions $p \in T'$ that are not a boundary tile. 
    51		Hence, there are $\Omega(\vert T'\vert)=\Omega(\vert T\vert)$ many boundary tiles. 
    52		Because $P \subseteq A$, we obtain $\vert P\vert \leq \vert A\vert$ implying $\vert T\vert \in \Omega(\sqrt{\vert A\vert})\subseteq\Omega(\sqrt{\vert P\vert})$.
    53		Hence, the shape $P'$ constructed by the greedy algorithm has at least $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{1}{2}})$ boundary tiles. A similar surface-to-volume argument implies a lower bound of $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{2}{3}})$ in the three-dimensional case. 
    54		
    55		Therefore, in dimension $d= 2,3$, this approach yields an approximation factor of $\nicefrac {\vert P'\vert}{\vert P\vert} = \Omega(n^{\nicefrac {-1} d})$.
    56	\end{proof}

===== 05-special.tex =====
     1	\section{Efficient algorithms for special classes of shapes}
     2	Due to the equivalence of construction and deconstruction, we pursue the goal of classifying tiles that can be removed from a shape, without harming its deconstructibility. In general, this seems to be really difficult. On the one hand, it is not sufficient to restrict the search for removable tiles to \emph{corners} (tiles with exactly one horizontal and one vertical neighbor), because for successfully deconstructing a polyomino, it may be necessary to remove non-corner tiles first, see \cref{fig:problempolyomino1}.
     3	On the other hand, removing non-corner tiles can result in an indestructible subshape, again see \cref{fig:problempolyomino1}.
     4	Furthermore, even in simple polyominoes, the removal of a corner tile can result in an indestructible subshape, see \cref{fig:problempolyomino2}.
     5	Note that the latter is not the case in the full tilt model~\cite{becker2018tilt}.
     6	
     7	\begin{figure}[h]
     8		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.5\textwidth}
     9			\centering
    10			\includegraphics[page=11]{figures/figs-final}
    11			\caption{}
    12			\label{fig:problempolyomino1}
    13		\end{subfigure}\hfill
    14		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.5\textwidth}
    15			\centering
    16			\includegraphics[page=12]{figures/figs-final}
    17			\caption{}
    18			\label{fig:problempolyomino2}
    19		\end{subfigure}
    20		\caption{(a) No corner tile (dark gray) can be removed, because they either do not have a deconstruction step or are essential for connectivity. 
    21			Successively removing the tiles~1, 2, and 3 by suitable deconstruction steps, the obtained  shape can easily be deconstructed.
    22			Removing the red tiles first results in an indestructible shape.
    23			(b) By removing $a$ first, we can remove the spiral starting with $c$ and afterwards the rest. By removing $b$ first, the spiral starting with $d$ can be removed, but the remaining shape is indestructible.}
    24		\label{fig:problempolyomino}
    25	\end{figure}
    26	
    27	Therefore we consider special classes of shapes and show that the problem becomes easy in the case of trees and scaled shapes.
    28	
    29	\subsection{Tree shapes}
    30	We show that \problemtitle can be decided in linear time (in the size of the shape) for the class of tree-shapes by a greedy algorithm. We show this in detail for the two-dimensional setting, i.e., for tree-shaped polyominoes. The results can simply be adapted for tree-shaped polycubes in three dimensions. Because the removal of a tile with more than one neighbor results in splitting the polyomino in several parts, we are restricted to remove tiles with exactly one neighbor, i.e, leaves. If there are any tiles left, but no further tile can be removed, we conclude that the polyomino cannot be constructed.
    31	
    32	We begin by stating two facts about removable tiles. Firstly, by removing a removable tile, other tiles do not lose their property of being removable. And secondly, if a tree-shaped polyomino is constructible, then after removing any removable tile, the resulting polyomino is also constructible.
    33	
    34	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tree:property}
    35		Let $P$ be a tree-shaped polyomino and $\mathcal{R}_P$ the set of removable tiles of $P$. For all $P'\subseteq P$ it holds that if $t\in \mathcal{R}_P \cap P'$, then $t\in \mathcal{R}_{P'}$.
    36	\end{lemma}
    37	
    38	\begin{proof}
    39		Let $t, t' \in \mathcal{R}_P$ and $P' := P\setminus t'$. Due to the definition of $\mathcal{R}_P$, $P'$ is connected. Furthermore, the path that removes $t$ from $P$ still exists, because the removal of $t'$ cannot block this path. Therefore, $t \in \mathcal{R}_{P'}$.
    40	\end{proof}
    41	
    42	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:constructible-tree}
    43		Let $P$ be a constructible tree-shaped polyomino and let $t$ be a removable tile.
    44		Then $P\setminus t$ is also constructible.
    45	\end{lemma}
    46	
    47	\begin{proof}
    48		For the sake of a contradiction, let $P'\subsetneq P$ be the largest polyomino that results by removing tiles from $P$ such that $t\in P'$ and $P'\setminus t$ is constructible. 
    49		Let $t'$ be the last tile that was removed in this process to obtain $P'$ from $P$. 
    50		Because of \cref{lem:tree:property}, a feasible deconstruction sequence can contain two subsequent deconstruction steps so that the first one removes $t$ from the shape $P'\cup t'$, followed by removing $t'$. 
    51		Thus, $P'$ was not the largest constructible polyomino, which is a contradiction.
    52	\end{proof}
    53	
    54	By using \cref{lem:constructible-tree} iteratively, we obtain a simple strategy that decides whether a tree-shaped polyomino is constructible or not. By applying suitable subroutines and data structures, this yields a linear-time algorithm.
    55	
    56	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:tree}
    57		Let $P$ be a tree-shaped polyomino of size $n$. It can be decided in $O(n)$ time whether or not $P$ is constructible.
    58	\end{theorem}
    59	
    60	\begin{proof}
    61		Notice that every free position within the workspace that has an $L_\infty$-distance greater than 2 to any tile of the shape has no effect on a potential indestructibility of that shape. 
    62		Thus, to keep track of the tiles that are and that become removable, we just have to consider every position within an $L_\infty$-distance of at most 2 to any tile of the shape. 
    63		These positions are marked as \texttt{blocked}. 
    64		Note that there are at most $O(n)$ \texttt{blocked} positions, because each tile has at most 24 positions in a distance of at most 2.
    65		
    66		\texttt{Blocked} positions that are not adjacent to $P$, but are reachable from the outside of its bounding box are marked as \texttt{unblocked}. This can be done by a simple graph scan algorithm like BFS or DFS in $O(n)$ time.
    67		
    68		To identify whether a tile is removable, we only need to check the following:
    69		(i)~$t$ is a leaf tile, and
    70		(ii)~at least one position of $N[t]$ is adjacent to an \texttt{unblocked} position. 
    71		This procedure costs $O(1)$ time per tile; thus $O(n)$ time in total.
    72		\begin{figure}
    73			\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.49\textwidth}\centering
    74				\includegraphics[page=14]{figures/figs-final}
    75				\caption{}
    76				\label{fig:leaf-removal-1}
    77			\end{subfigure}
    78			\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.49\textwidth}\centering
    79				\includegraphics[page=15]{figures/figs-final}
    80				\caption{}
    81				\label{fig:leaf-removal-2}
    82			\end{subfigure}
    83			\caption{
    84				(a) A polyomino $P$ (gray) and the dual graph of $P$ (black). 
    85				Tiles in green are removable, and tiles in orange are leaves that are not removable yet. 
    86				Cyan dots represent free positions with the state \texttt{unblocked}, cyan circles represent free positions with the state \texttt{blocked}. 
    87				(b) The situation after the removal of the green tile in (a).}
    88			\label{fig:leaf-removal}
    89		\end{figure}
    90		
    91		The following steps are executed iteratively, until either all tiles have been removed, or no further tile can be removed from $P$. For this, let $t\in P$ be a removable tile.
    92		\begin{description}
    93			\item[Step 1:] Remove $t$ from $P$ and mark its former position as \texttt{blocked}.
    94			\item[Step 2:] \label{algo:tree:2} For each free position $p\in N[t]$ that is adjacent to an \texttt{unblocked} position:
    95			\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
    96				\item \label{algo:tree:2:a} Mark $p$ as \texttt{unblocked} if $N[p]$ is free and start a graph scan on \texttt{blocked} positions as well as mark them as \texttt{unblocked}, if they are reachable from $p$ and their respective neighborhood is free.
    97				\item \label{algo:tree:2:b} During the scan, if a \texttt{blocked} position does not change its state, check whether adjacent tiles become removable.
    98			\end{enumerate}
    99		\end{description}
   100		If there are tiles left that cannot be removed, we conclude that $P$ is not constructible. Otherwise, $P$ is constructible and the output is a feasible construction sequence.
   101		
   102		Each free position can change its state only once. 
   103		We charge the constant cost of the checking part in  
   104		Step 2(b) of the algorithm to the respective adjacent \texttt{unblocked} position. 
   105		Thus, over all iterations, each position is charged $O(1)$ cost, yielding a runtime of $O(n)$.
   106	\end{proof}
   107	
   108	It is easy to see that the same holds true for tree-shapes in 3D.
   109	
   110	\begin{corollary}\label{cor:tree}
   111		Let $P$ be a tree-shaped polycube of size $n$. It can be decided in $O(n)$ time whether or not $P$ is constructible.
   112	\end{corollary}
   113	
   114	\subsection{Scaled shapes}
   115	In the previous section we showed that it can be decided efficiently whether or not a tree-shape is constructible. 
   116	A crucial point is that these shapes are thin. 
   117	A simple example for a non-constructible tree-shape is a slightly modified \emph{Hilbert curve} (in fact it is even a path) in that both endpoints are not removable, see \cref{fig:hilbert-curve}. 
   118	Therefore, we want to assume that we are allowed to construct a \emph{scaled copy} of the actual shape.
   119	
   120	\begin{figure}
   121		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.47\textwidth}\centering
   122			\includegraphics[page=18]{figures/figs-final}
   123			\caption{}
   124			\label{fig:hilbert2d}
   125		\end{subfigure}
   126		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.52\textwidth}\centering
   127			\includegraphics[scale=0.045]{figures/Hilbert_bg.jpg}
   128			\caption{}
   129			\label{fig:hilbert3d}
   130		\end{subfigure}
   131		\caption{(a) A (slightly modified) 2D Hilbert curve that cannot be constructed because both endpoints are not removable. (b) A (slightly modified) 3D Hilbert curve that cannot be constructed; the two leaves are represented as luminous cubes.}
   132		\label{fig:hilbert-curve}
   133	\end{figure}
   134	
   135	\begin{definition}
   136		Let $P$ be a polyomino and $c\in \mathbb{N}$. By $P^c$ we denote the $c$-scaled copy of $P$, i.e., each tile in $P$ is replaced by a $c\times c$ square of tiles.
   137	\end{definition}
   138	
   139	We show that the 2-scaled copy of a non-degenerate polyomino is constructible.
   140	Recall that in a non-degenerate polyomino $P$, for every non-adjacent pair $t_1, t_2\in P$ of tiles with $\vert N[t_1]\cap N[t_2]\vert \neq \emptyset$, there is at least one occupied position $p\in N[t_1]\cap N[t_2]$, i.e., the tiles $t_1, t_2$ have a common neighbor.
   141	Note that no scaling factor suffices to guarantee constructibility for degenerate polyominoes, see \cref{fig:scaling-degenerate-shape}. 
   142	
   143	\begin{figure}[ht]
   144		\centering
   145		\includegraphics[page=13, scale=0.8]{figures/figs-final}
   146		\caption{For deconstruction, it is necessary to remove at least one of the red tiles. Independent from the scaling factor, both tiles block each other and we are only able to deconstruct a staircase to the right and below both tiles (hatched part).}
   147		\label{fig:scaling-degenerate-shape}
   148	\end{figure}
   149	
   150	\begin{definition}\label{def:empty}
   151		We call a polyomino $P$ $c$-\emph{empty}, if for every pair $(p_i, p_j)$ of free positions in the same connected component of $G_{\mathbb{Z}^2\setminus P}$ there is a square of size $c\times c$ that initially overlaps with $p_i$ can be moved in such a way that it eventually overlaps with $p_j$ without overlapping with any position of $P$ at any time. 
   152		We call $P$ \emph{weakly $c$-empty} if at any time at most one corner of the square overlaps with a position of $P$.
   153	\end{definition}
   154	
   155	For an illustration of \cref{def:empty}, see \cref{fig:decomp_empty}\subref{fig:empty}. 
   156	Note that in the case of weakly 3-emptiness it is sufficient to ensure that during the motion of a tile its neighborhood $N[\cdot]$ is kept empty. However, it is not possible to limit the definition of weakly 3-emptiness to this, because then free positions that are adjacent to two tiles (especially ``corner positions'' in the free space) would be excluded from this definition.
   157	For a $3$-empty polyomino $P$, it is straightforward to see that as long as a tile $t\in P$ can be moved to a free position $p$ that lies in the outer face, such that all surrounding positions of $p$ are free as well, $t$ can be removed from $P$. 
   158	The intuition is the following: Consider a path of a $3\times 3$-square, centered at that free position $p$, that connects $p$ to the outside of the bounding box of $P$. The deconstruction step for the tile $t$ consists of all positions given by that path.
   159	Note that this still holds if we consider weakly 3-empty polyominoes.
   160	
   161	We show that such a removable tile can always be found if $P$ is the 2-scaled copy of a non-degenerate polyomino.
   162	One of the core ideas of our method is to make $P$ weakly 3-empty.
   163	If $P$ is weakly 3-empty, we consider a partition of $P$ into horizontal \emph{slabs}.
   164	Based on this partition, we show that either a leaf of the dual graph of the partition can be removed, or a hole of $P$ can be \emph{cut open}, i.e., two adjacent tiles of $P$ can be removed to reduce the number of holes by 1.
   165	
   166	\begin{definition}
   167		A \emph{slice} of a shape $P$ is the set of all tiles sharing the same $y$-coordinate.
   168		A~\emph{slab} is a maximal connected set of tiles within a slice.
   169	\end{definition}
   170	
   171	\begin{definition}
   172		Let $P$ be a shape, and $\mathcal S_P$ its partition into slabs.
   173		For 2D shapes, or 3D shapes, we refer by $\mathcal C(\mathcal S_P)$ to the edge-contact graph, or to the face-contact graph of $\mathcal S_P$, respectively. That is, each slab is represented by a vertex, and two vertices are connected if and only if the union of both slabs is connected.
   174	\end{definition}
   175	
   176	\begin{figure}
   177		\centering
   178		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.45\textwidth}
   179			\centering
   180			\includegraphics[page=4,scale=0.7]{figures/figs-final}
   181			\caption{}
   182			\label{fig:horizontal_decomp}
   183		\end{subfigure}\hfill
   184		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.55\textwidth}
   185			\centering
   186			\includegraphics[page=5,scale=0.7]{figures/figs-final}
   187			\caption{}
   188			\label{fig:empty}
   189		\end{subfigure}
   190		\caption{(a) The $3$-scaled copy $P^3$ of a polyomino $P$ (gray tiles), its partition into horizontal slabs $\mathcal{S}_P$, and its dual graph $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_P)$ (dark gray). (b) A polyomino that is 3-empty, weakly 3-empty, and not (weakly) 3-empty, respectively.}
   191		\label{fig:decomp_empty}
   192	\end{figure}
   193	
   194	It is straightforward to see that any leaf of $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_P)$ can be removed if it lies in the outer face and the polyomino is weakly 3-empty.
   195	
   196	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:scaling:weakly}
   197		Let $P$ be a non-degenerate, weakly 3-empty polyomino. If $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{S}_P)$ contains a~leaf that lies in the outer face, then the corresponding slab~$S$ can be removed from~$P$.
   198	\end{lemma}
   199	
   200	\begin{proof}
   201		Without loss of generality, we assume that there is no neighbor below $S$ and that $P\setminus t$ is connected, where $t$ is the rightmost tile of $S$. %Arguments for the leftmost tile or if $S$ has a neighbor below but no neighbor above are analogous.
   202		Analogous arguments hold in case that $t$ is the leftmost tile of a slab $S$.
   203		
   204		Consider position of $t$ and the position $p$ which lies one step below and one step to the right of $t$. 
   205		Because $S$ is weakly 3-empty, $p$ is free and it is the center of a $3\times 3$ square that contains $t$ in the top left corner.
   206		Because $P$ is non-degenerate and by assumption the slab $S$ has no neighbor to the bottom side, we can move $t$ to $p$ by moving it one step down and then one step right. 
   207		Because $P$ is weakly 3-empty, $t$ can be removed. %From there, we can move $t$ away from $P\setminus t$ by following the path of the $3\times 3$ square.
   208		It is straightforward to see that $P\setminus t$ is still weakly 3-empty.
   209		
   210		Because we can iteratively remove the leftmost or the rightmost tile of $S$ without losing connectivity, we eventually remove $S$ from $P$.
   211	\end{proof}
   212	
   213	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:scaling:simple:2D}
   214		For every non-degenerate polyomino $P$, its 2-scaled copy $P^2$ is constructible.
   215	\end{theorem}
   216	
   217	\begin{proof}
   218		The approach works in two phases. 
   219		The first phase removes tiles from $P^2$ such that the remaining shape is weakly 3-empty. 
   220		The second phase deconstructs the remaining shape slab by slab. 
   221		Any holes that may be contained in the shape are cut open at appropriate times within the deconstruction sequence at suitable positions. 
   222		If necessary, these phases are executed again one after the other.
   223		
   224		Phase 1 works as follows, see~\cref{fig:scale_fac2}.
   225		Consider the set $F$ that contains all free positions that can participate in a pair with a free position outside the bounding box of $P^2$, such that these pairs fulfill the weakly 3-empty property. 
   226		We will describe how tiles of $P^2$ can be removed such that any position lying in the outer face will belong to this set.
   227		
   228		As long as there are adjacent free positions that are not in $F$, repeat the following:
   229		Let $p_1,p_2\notin F$ be two adjacent free positions that both have a neighbor in $F$, say $p'_1$ and $p'_2$ respectively.
   230		Without loss of generality, let $(p_1,p_2)$ be a vertical pair and let the neighbors be in the left direction.
   231		Then, to the top and to the bottom of $(p'_1,p'_2)$ there are tiles $t_t,t_b\in P^2$ (or else $p_1, p_2\in F$).
   232		Consider the maximal horizontal line $L$ of $m$ tiles $(t_1,\dots,t_m)$ ordered from left to right with $t_b\in L$ that have a free position to the top.
   233		We make the following case distinction:
   234		\begin{description}
   235			\item[{Case} 1:] $t_b \neq t_1$: Remove the tile $t$ that is right of $t_b$ by moving $t$ two steps to the top. This position is the position to the left of $p'_2$, i.e., a position from $F$. Furthermore, this position has to be the center of a $3\times 3$ square containing only $t_t$.
   236			Thus, $t$ can be removed from $P^2$.
   237			After $t$ is removed, $t_b$ can be moved one step to the top, followed by a step right and a step up, reaching the exact same position.\label{item:2scaled2}
   238			\item[{Case} 2:] $t_b = t_1$: Remove $t_b$ by moving the tile one step to the top, followed by a step to the right.\label{item:2scaled1}
   239		\end{description}
   240		After removing $t_b$, we can proceed by iteratively removing $L$.
   241		Note that in some cases $t_1$ or~$t_m$ may not be removable, because they lie in a corner.
   242		However, the free positions adjacent to the top of these tiles will be contained in $F$, and thus, the corridor is wide enough.
   243		
   244		The cases when $p_1,p_2$ have their neighbors from $F$ in the direction to the right, or when they are a horizontal pair, are handled analogously. 
   245		To maintain connectivity, we always choose the line $L$ in a way that $L$ is to the left, or to the down direction of $(p'_1,p'_2)$.
   246		
   247		For Phase 2 we can assume that the remaining polyomino $P'$ is weakly 3-empty. Thus, we can apply Lemma~\ref{lem:scaling:weakly} to remove slabs that correspond to leaves.
   248		If there is no such slab, then we either removed every single tile, or there is a slab $S$ incident to the outer face with $k$ neighbors in $\mathcal C(\mathcal S_{P'})$ on one side only, whose removal would produce at most $k-1$ connected components.
   249		This slab must exist. 
   250		To see this, consider the set of all slabs  that have their neighbors in $\mathcal C(\mathcal S_{P'})$ on one side only, and consider a maximal path in $\mathcal C(\mathcal S_{P'})$ between these slabs. 
   251		Then the slab $S'$ is a slab with the desired property, or else we can extend the path.
   252		
   253		Now, consider a slab $S$ as defined above.
   254		We know that there are two slabs $S_1$ and $S_2$ adjacent to $S$ that belong to the same connected component in $P'\setminus S$.
   255		Without loss of generality, let $S_1$ be to the left of $S_2$.
   256		The first two tiles $t_1$ and $t_2$ from $S$ that have a larger $x$-coordinate than the rightmost tile in $S_1$ can be removed.
   257		There cannot be any tile above or below $t_1$ and $t_2$, because we consider the 2-scaled copy of a polyomino, and thus, this cannot break any connectivity.
   258		Also, any slab connected to $S$ to the left of $t_1$ remains connected to any slab that is connected to $S$ to the right of $t_2$.
   259		This implies that $P'\setminus\{t_1, t_2\}$ is a connected polyomino.
   260		
   261		However, $P'\setminus\{t_1, t_2\}$ may 
   262		no longer be weakly 3-empty.
   263		By restarting Phase 1, further tiles can be removed.
   264		Because tiles are getting removed in each case, $P^2$ is eventually deconstructed. Thus, we conclude that for every initial polyomino $P$, its $2$-scaled copy $P^2$ is constructible.
   265	\end{proof}
   266	
