   288	
   289	Because there are (even tree-shaped) indestructible polyominoes, this result is tight. 
   290	If the polyomino is already 3-empty, we can skip the first phase. 
   291	Whenever we have to cut open a hole, we remove three (instead of two) tiles, such that the property of being 3-empty is preserved.
   292	This results in the following corollary.
   293	
   294	\begin{corollary}\label{lem:scaling:simple:3-empty}
   295		Every non-degenerate, 3-empty polyomino is constructible.
   296	\end{corollary}
   297	
   298	
   299	In the three-dimensional setting we cannot simply widen narrow corridors when scaled by a factor of 2, e.g., imagine a tube with an inner diameter of 2.
   300	Therefore, the approach in 2D does not work in 3D. 
   301	However, we show that a scaling factor of 3 suffices to guarantee constructibility.
   302	
   303	\begin{definition}
   304		Let $S$ be a slab of a 3D shape, and $S_1, S_2$ be two slabs adjacent to~$S$.
   305		$S_1$ is called \emph{ring} if  there are tiles of $S_1$ bounding a (3D) hole of $P$.
   306		$S_1$ and $S_2$ build a \emph{loop} (or 2D hole), if both are in the same connected component of $P\setminus S$.
   307		If both cases do not apply, $S_1$ is called a \emph{pillar}.
   308	\end{definition}
   309	
   310	Using the same arguments as in the 2D case, we obtain the following lemma.
   311	
   312	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:scaling:loop}
   313		Let $P$ be a 3D shape and $\mathcal{S}_P$ a decomposition of $P$ into slabs.
   314		If $\mathcal{S}_P$ contains no slab of degree one, then there exists a slab accessible from the outer face with at most one pillar (outside a 3D hole).
   315	\end{lemma}
   316	
   317	\begin{proof}
   318		Consider the set of all slabs adjacent to the outer face, that have their neighbors on one side only.
   319		In this set, there is a maximal path of these slabs using pillars that are adjacent to the outer face.
   320		Because $P$ is finite, the start and end slab of this path can only have one pillar.
   321	\end{proof}
   322	
   323	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:scaling:simple:3D}
   324		For every non-degenerate polycube $P$, its 3-scaled copy $P^3$ is constructible.
   325	\end{theorem}
   326	
   327	\begin{proof}
   328		Similar to the 2D case, we proceed in two phases.
   329		In Phase 1, we successively remove slabs that are leaves in $\mathcal C(\mathcal S_P)$.
   330		Because we consider the 3-scaled copy of a shape, there is always sufficient space to remove all tiles from these slabs (see also 2D algorithm). 
   331		If every slab has degree at least two, we proceed with Phase 2. Note that leaves can still exist, but these are not adjacent to the outer face and therefore not removable.
   332		
   333		In Phase 2, we first search for a slab $S$ that (i) is adjacent to the outer face, (ii) has neighbors  on one side only, and (iii) is adjacent to a ring $S_r$.
   334		Consider a hole $H$ that is bounded by tiles of $S$ and $S_r$.
   335		Because $S$ has neighbors on one side only, $S$ covers one face of $H$ completely.
   336		Let $S_r^\downarrow$ be the tiles from $S$ that are adjacent to $S_r$.
   337		Then a $3\times 3$ square from $S$ that is adjacent to $H$ and $S_r^\downarrow$ can be removed~(see also \cref{fig:3D_scaling_defs_a}).
   338		
   339		In the case that $S$ does not exist, then, because of \cref{lem:scaling:loop}, there must be at least one slab $S'$ with at least one adjacent loop.
   340		Let $S_1, S_2$ be two slabs adjacent to $S'$ building a loop, and let $S_1', S_2'$ be the set of tiles from $S'$ adjacent to $S_1$ and $S_2$, respectively.
   341		Consider the set $C$ of maximal connected components of $S\setminus \{S_1', S_2'\}$.
   342		The components of $C$ can be divided into the following three types (see also \cref{fig:3D_scaling_defs_b}):
   343		
   344		\begin{description}
   345			\item[{Type} 1:] Components that are adjacent only to tiles that belong to $S_1'$.
   346			\item[{Type} 2:] Components that are adjacent only to tiles that belong to $S_2'$.
   347			\item[{Type} 3:] Components that are adjacent only to tiles that belong to both $S_1'$ and $S_2'$.
   348		\end{description}
   349		
   350		\begin{figure}
   351			\centering
   352			\begin{subfigure}{.49\columnwidth}
   353				\includegraphics[scale=.5]{./figures/3D_scaling_hole.pdf}
   354				\caption{}
   355				\label{fig:3D_scaling_defs_a}
   356			\end{subfigure}\hfill
   357			\begin{subfigure}{.49\columnwidth}
   358				\includegraphics[scale=.5]{./figures/3D_scaling_defs.pdf}
   359				\caption{}
   360				\label{fig:3D_scaling_defs_b}
   361			\end{subfigure}
   362			\caption{
   363				(a) A slab $S$ with tiles $S_r^\downarrow$ adjacent to a ring (shown in blue) and adjacent to a hole~(green).
   364				We remove a $3\times 3$ square from the green area (shown in red).
   365				Gray area is part of $S$ but is not needed to consider here.
   366				(b) Blue area corresponds to slabs $S_1$ and $S_2$, dark blue area corresponds to tiles from $S$, that are below $S_1$ and $S_2$, i.e., $S_1'$ and $S_2'$.
   367				The orange area corresponds to a connected component of Type 1, red areas to components of Type 2, and the green areas to components of Type 3.
   368				Gray squares indicate loops, when connected by a dotted curve.}
   369			\label{fig:3D_scaling_defs}
   370		\end{figure}
   371		
   372		Any component that has no adjacent slab above/below itself can be removed.
   373		In the case that thereby all components of Type 3 have been removed, we proceed again with Phase 1.
   374		Otherwise, we make the following case distinction:
   375		
   376		\begin{description}
   377			\item[{Case} 1:] Only components of Type 1 and Type 3 are still present.
   378			In this case, we remove $S_2'$ and all tiles adjacent to $S_2'$. 
   379			This keeps the remaining shape connected and non-degenerate, while also giving sufficient space to remove $S_2$ if it becomes a degree-one slab.
   380			%		Also notice that we are still 3-empty with one loop removed.
   381			
   382			\item[{Case} 2:] Only components of Type~2 and Type~3 are still present.
   383			This is analogue to Case~1.
   384			
   385			\item[{Case} 3:] Only components of Type~3 are present.
   386			This is analogue to Case~1.
   387			
   388			\item[{Case} 4:] Components of all three types are still present.
   389			Without loss of generality, assume that the only pillar (if existing) is adjacent to a component of Type~1 or Type~3; otherwise we switch $S_1$ and $S_2$.
   390			Consider all pairs of slabs adjacent to $S'$ that build a loop.
   391			Suppose there is a loop build by slabs $\bar S_1$ and $\bar S_2$ both adjacent to components of Type~2 (see rightmost gray squares in the red area in \cref{fig:3D_scaling_defs_b}).
   392			Then, we restart Phase~2 with slab $S'$, and the loop build by $\bar S_1$ and $\bar S_2$, such that the former slabs $S_1$ and $S_2$ are then adjacent to components of Type~1 or Type~3.
   393			This ensures that we do not cycle back to these two slabs.
   394			
   395			We repeat this procedure until we have the following situation:
   396			(i) Every loop beginning in a component of Type~2 has its end in a component of Type~1 or Type~3, and (ii) the only pillar (if existing) is connected to a component of Type~1 or Type~3.
   397			With that, we can simply remove any component of Type~2 without losing connectivity, and we can continue with Case~1.
   398		\end{description}
   399		In each iteration of this phase, at least one loop is getting removed or one hole is cut open.
   400		Therefore, Phase 2 can only have finitely many iterations and thus eventually terminates.
   401		Combining both phases shows that the 3-scaled copy of every non-degenerate 3D shape is constructible.
   402	\end{proof}
   403	
   404	Unlike as in the 2D case, we cannot show that this result is tight and conjecture that even the 2-scaled copy of every non-degenerate polycube is constructible.

