     9			\caption{}
    10			\label{fig:approx1}
    11		\end{subfigure}\hfill
    12		\begin{subfigure}{0.33\textwidth}
    13			\includegraphics[page=2, scale=0.85]{figures/figs-final}
    14			\caption{}
    15			\label{fig:approx2}
    16		\end{subfigure}\hfill
    17		\begin{subfigure}{0.33\textwidth}
    18			\includegraphics[page=3, scale=0.85]{figures/figs-final}
    19			\caption{}
    20			\label{fig:approx3}
    21		\end{subfigure}
    22		\caption{(a) Boundary tiles (dark cyan) and non-boundary tiles (gray). (b) Every step of the  algorithm adds a tile in two steps: (1)~Move a new tile $t$ to a boundary position $p$ of $P$ that is free. (2)~Move $t$ on $P$ from $p$ to a position that is adjacent to a tile of the current polyomino $P'$. (c) The curve $B$ (blue), and the set $T$ (orange).}
    23		\label{fig:approx}
    24	\end{figure}
    25	
    26	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:constructability}
    27		In dimension $d=2, 3$, the greedy algorithm is an $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{-1}{d}})$-approximation for \textsc{MaxSTAP}.
    28	\end{theorem}
    29	
    30	\begin{proof}
    31		We prove the theorem by showing that greedily filling up accessible free positions leads to a polyomino $P' \subseteq P$ with $\nicefrac{\vert P'\vert}{\vert P\vert} \in \Omega(n^{\nicefrac{-1}{d}})$. 
    32		A position $p$ is \emph{accessible} with respect to a polyomino $P$ if we can move a tile $t$ to this position such that $t$~is never adjacent to a tile of $P$ unless $t$ lies on $p$. A tile $p \in P$ is a \emph{boundary tile} of $P$ if $p$ is accessible with respect to $P \setminus p$, see \cref{fig:approx1}. 
    33		If there is a boundary tile $p$ of $P$ that is not part of $P'$, we add a new tile $t$ to $P'$ in two steps, see \cref{fig:approx2}: 
    34		(1)~We move $t$ to the position $p$. 
    35		(2)~We move $t$ on $P$ to a position adjacent to a tile of $P'$. This implies that the greedy algorithm ends up with a polyomino holding all boundary tiles of $P$.
    36		
    37		Next, we show a lower bound on the boundary tiles of $P$ of $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{(d-1)}{d}})$.
    38		We will only show this for dimension $d=2$, %a 
    39		similar %surface-volume-
    40		arguments hold for $d=3$. 
    41		Let $B$~be the union of all edges lying between an accessible and a non-accessible position with respect to $P$, see blue curve in \cref{fig:approx3}.
    42		$B$ is a non-self-intersecting curve by the definition of accessible positions. 
    43		Thus, $B$ partitions the plane into a bounded area $A$ containing $P$ and an unbounded area. 
    44		Let $T$ be the union of all positions from $A$ sharing at least a corner with $B$, see the orange positions in \cref{fig:approx3}. 
    45		Then $\vert T\vert \geq \sqrt{\vert A\vert}$. 
    46		Note that not each position of $T$ is occupied by~$P$, see the light gray positions in \cref{fig:approx3}. 
    47		Let $T'$ be the positions along $T$ that share an edge with $B$. It is easy to see that $2\vert T'\vert\geq \vert T\vert$.
    48		Each position $p$ from $T'$ that is not a boundary tile from $P$ is adjacent to a boundary tile $p'$ from $P$.
    49		We call $p'$ a \emph{blocking tile} of $p$. 
    50		Each boundary tile is a blocking tile for a constant number of positions $p \in T'$ that are not a boundary tile. 
    51		Hence, there are $\Omega(\vert T'\vert)=\Omega(\vert T\vert)$ many boundary tiles. 
    52		Because $P \subseteq A$, we obtain $\vert P\vert \leq \vert A\vert$ implying $\vert T\vert \in \Omega(\sqrt{\vert A\vert})\subseteq\Omega(\sqrt{\vert P\vert})$.
    53		Hence, the shape $P'$ constructed by the greedy algorithm has at least $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{1}{2}})$ boundary tiles. A similar surface-to-volume argument implies a lower bound of $\Omega(n^{\nicefrac{2}{3}})$ in the three-dimensional case. 
    54		
    55		Therefore, in dimension $d= 2,3$, this approach yields an approximation factor of $\nicefrac {\vert P'\vert}{\vert P\vert} = \Omega(n^{\nicefrac {-1} d})$.
    56	\end{proof}

===== SPECIAL 1-150 =====
     1	\section{Efficient algorithms for special classes of shapes}
     2	Due to the equivalence of construction and deconstruction, we pursue the goal of classifying tiles that can be removed from a shape, without harming its deconstructibility. In general, this seems to be really difficult. On the one hand, it is not sufficient to restrict the search for removable tiles to \emph{corners} (tiles with exactly one horizontal and one vertical neighbor), because for successfully deconstructing a polyomino, it may be necessary to remove non-corner tiles first, see \cref{fig:problempolyomino1}.
     3	On the other hand, removing non-corner tiles can result in an indestructible subshape, again see \cref{fig:problempolyomino1}.
     4	Furthermore, even in simple polyominoes, the removal of a corner tile can result in an indestructible subshape, see \cref{fig:problempolyomino2}.
     5	Note that the latter is not the case in the full tilt model~\cite{becker2018tilt}.
     6	
     7	\begin{figure}[h]
     8		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.5\textwidth}
     9			\centering
    10			\includegraphics[page=11]{figures/figs-final}
    11			\caption{}
    12			\label{fig:problempolyomino1}
    13		\end{subfigure}\hfill
    14		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.5\textwidth}
    15			\centering
    16			\includegraphics[page=12]{figures/figs-final}
    17			\caption{}
    18			\label{fig:problempolyomino2}
    19		\end{subfigure}
    20		\caption{(a) No corner tile (dark gray) can be removed, because they either do not have a deconstruction step or are essential for connectivity. 
    21			Successively removing the tiles~1, 2, and 3 by suitable deconstruction steps, the obtained  shape can easily be deconstructed.
    22			Removing the red tiles first results in an indestructible shape.
    23			(b) By removing $a$ first, we can remove the spiral starting with $c$ and afterwards the rest. By removing $b$ first, the spiral starting with $d$ can be removed, but the remaining shape is indestructible.}
    24		\label{fig:problempolyomino}
    25	\end{figure}
    26	
    27	Therefore we consider special classes of shapes and show that the problem becomes easy in the case of trees and scaled shapes.
    28	
    29	\subsection{Tree shapes}
    30	We show that \problemtitle can be decided in linear time (in the size of the shape) for the class of tree-shapes by a greedy algorithm. We show this in detail for the two-dimensional setting, i.e., for tree-shaped polyominoes. The results can simply be adapted for tree-shaped polycubes in three dimensions. Because the removal of a tile with more than one neighbor results in splitting the polyomino in several parts, we are restricted to remove tiles with exactly one neighbor, i.e, leaves. If there are any tiles left, but no further tile can be removed, we conclude that the polyomino cannot be constructed.
    31	
    32	We begin by stating two facts about removable tiles. Firstly, by removing a removable tile, other tiles do not lose their property of being removable. And secondly, if a tree-shaped polyomino is constructible, then after removing any removable tile, the resulting polyomino is also constructible.
    33	
    34	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tree:property}
    35		Let $P$ be a tree-shaped polyomino and $\mathcal{R}_P$ the set of removable tiles of $P$. For all $P'\subseteq P$ it holds that if $t\in \mathcal{R}_P \cap P'$, then $t\in \mathcal{R}_{P'}$.
    36	\end{lemma}
    37	
    38	\begin{proof}
    39		Let $t, t' \in \mathcal{R}_P$ and $P' := P\setminus t'$. Due to the definition of $\mathcal{R}_P$, $P'$ is connected. Furthermore, the path that removes $t$ from $P$ still exists, because the removal of $t'$ cannot block this path. Therefore, $t \in \mathcal{R}_{P'}$.
    40	\end{proof}
    41	
    42	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:constructible-tree}
    43		Let $P$ be a constructible tree-shaped polyomino and let $t$ be a removable tile.
    44		Then $P\setminus t$ is also constructible.
    45	\end{lemma}
    46	
    47	\begin{proof}
    48		For the sake of a contradiction, let $P'\subsetneq P$ be the largest polyomino that results by removing tiles from $P$ such that $t\in P'$ and $P'\setminus t$ is constructible. 
    49		Let $t'$ be the last tile that was removed in this process to obtain $P'$ from $P$. 
    50		Because of \cref{lem:tree:property}, a feasible deconstruction sequence can contain two subsequent deconstruction steps so that the first one removes $t$ from the shape $P'\cup t'$, followed by removing $t'$. 
    51		Thus, $P'$ was not the largest constructible polyomino, which is a contradiction.
    52	\end{proof}
    53	
    54	By using \cref{lem:constructible-tree} iteratively, we obtain a simple strategy that decides whether a tree-shaped polyomino is constructible or not. By applying suitable subroutines and data structures, this yields a linear-time algorithm.
    55	
    56	\begin{theorem}\label{thm:tree}
    57		Let $P$ be a tree-shaped polyomino of size $n$. It can be decided in $O(n)$ time whether or not $P$ is constructible.
    58	\end{theorem}
    59	
    60	\begin{proof}
    61		Notice that every free position within the workspace that has an $L_\infty$-distance greater than 2 to any tile of the shape has no effect on a potential indestructibility of that shape. 
    62		Thus, to keep track of the tiles that are and that become removable, we just have to consider every position within an $L_\infty$-distance of at most 2 to any tile of the shape. 
    63		These positions are marked as \texttt{blocked}. 
    64		Note that there are at most $O(n)$ \texttt{blocked} positions, because each tile has at most 24 positions in a distance of at most 2.
    65		
    66		\texttt{Blocked} positions that are not adjacent to $P$, but are reachable from the outside of its bounding box are marked as \texttt{unblocked}. This can be done by a simple graph scan algorithm like BFS or DFS in $O(n)$ time.
    67		
    68		To identify whether a tile is removable, we only need to check the following:
    69		(i)~$t$ is a leaf tile, and
    70		(ii)~at least one position of $N[t]$ is adjacent to an \texttt{unblocked} position. 
    71		This procedure costs $O(1)$ time per tile; thus $O(n)$ time in total.
    72		\begin{figure}
    73			\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.49\textwidth}\centering
    74				\includegraphics[page=14]{figures/figs-final}
    75				\caption{}
    76				\label{fig:leaf-removal-1}
    77			\end{subfigure}
    78			\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.49\textwidth}\centering
    79				\includegraphics[page=15]{figures/figs-final}
    80				\caption{}
    81				\label{fig:leaf-removal-2}
    82			\end{subfigure}
    83			\caption{
    84				(a) A polyomino $P$ (gray) and the dual graph of $P$ (black). 
    85				Tiles in green are removable, and tiles in orange are leaves that are not removable yet. 
    86				Cyan dots represent free positions with the state \texttt{unblocked}, cyan circles represent free positions with the state \texttt{blocked}. 
    87				(b) The situation after the removal of the green tile in (a).}
    88			\label{fig:leaf-removal}
    89		\end{figure}
    90		
    91		The following steps are executed iteratively, until either all tiles have been removed, or no further tile can be removed from $P$. For this, let $t\in P$ be a removable tile.
    92		\begin{description}
    93			\item[Step 1:] Remove $t$ from $P$ and mark its former position as \texttt{blocked}.
    94			\item[Step 2:] \label{algo:tree:2} For each free position $p\in N[t]$ that is adjacent to an \texttt{unblocked} position:
    95			\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
    96				\item \label{algo:tree:2:a} Mark $p$ as \texttt{unblocked} if $N[p]$ is free and start a graph scan on \texttt{blocked} positions as well as mark them as \texttt{unblocked}, if they are reachable from $p$ and their respective neighborhood is free.
    97				\item \label{algo:tree:2:b} During the scan, if a \texttt{blocked} position does not change its state, check whether adjacent tiles become removable.
    98			\end{enumerate}
    99		\end{description}
   100		If there are tiles left that cannot be removed, we conclude that $P$ is not constructible. Otherwise, $P$ is constructible and the output is a feasible construction sequence.
   101		
   102		Each free position can change its state only once. 
   103		We charge the constant cost of the checking part in  
   104		Step 2(b) of the algorithm to the respective adjacent \texttt{unblocked} position. 
   105		Thus, over all iterations, each position is charged $O(1)$ cost, yielding a runtime of $O(n)$.
   106	\end{proof}
   107	
   108	It is easy to see that the same holds true for tree-shapes in 3D.
   109	
   110	\begin{corollary}\label{cor:tree}
   111		Let $P$ be a tree-shaped polycube of size $n$. It can be decided in $O(n)$ time whether or not $P$ is constructible.
   112	\end{corollary}
   113	
   114	\subsection{Scaled shapes}
   115	In the previous section we showed that it can be decided efficiently whether or not a tree-shape is constructible. 
   116	A crucial point is that these shapes are thin. 
   117	A simple example for a non-constructible tree-shape is a slightly modified \emph{Hilbert curve} (in fact it is even a path) in that both endpoints are not removable, see \cref{fig:hilbert-curve}. 
   118	Therefore, we want to assume that we are allowed to construct a \emph{scaled copy} of the actual shape.
   119	
   120	\begin{figure}
   121		\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.47\textwidth}\centering
   122			\includegraphics[page=18]{figures/figs-final}
   123			\caption{}
   124			\label{fig:hilbert2d}
   125		\end{subfigure}
