06-conclusion.tex:1:\section{Conclusion and future work}
06-conclusion.tex:2:We provided a number of algorithmic results for assembling shapes by connecting particles one after the other to a fixed seed tile in a single step model. 
06-conclusion.tex:4:We showed that constructibility can be decided for special classes of shapes, but we do not know how hard it is to decide whether an arbitrary polyomino is constructible or not in the considered model, i.e., what is the computational complexity of 2D-\problemtitle? It seems that a similar construction as in the three-dimensional setting should work; however, the main difficulty is guaranteeing connectivity during a feasible deconstruction. We note that this is also an open question in the full tilt model~\cite{becker2018tilt}. Another question is whether 3D-\problemtitle remains NP-complete when restricted to the class of non-degenerate shapes.
06-conclusion.tex:7:If this assumption is relaxed, i.e., more than one tile at a time can be added to and controlled in the workspace, it is easy to see that more shapes are constructible, e.g., see \cref{fig:more-tiles-construction}.
06-conclusion.tex:9:This also leads to the question: ``Which shapes are constructible by using pre-assembled shapes (e.g., trominoes, tetrominoes, etc.)?''
06-conclusion.tex:10:By taking this a step further, we could also ask for a staged approach similar to~\cite{schmidt2018efficient}, where whole subassemblies can attach to each other. Another question arises by considering multiple seed tiles. What classes of shapes are constructible, if multiple seed tiles can be placed in advance, again see~\cref{fig:more-tiles-construction}.
